                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "wkdyB",
                      "name": "Notes",
                      "fill": "#1A1400",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "A WebGL shader draws a gas planet in slate and bone with one dark eddy below the equator. The light sits low and to the upper right, so a third of the disc is in shadow.\n\nFour alternating jets drag the cloud pattern sideways. The globe turns once every 90 seconds and the clouds drift slowly, so the scene looks almost still.\n\nChecking: over 4 seconds the light swings round to face the sign-in block, the planet moves 6% closer, and the eddy spins faster the longer it waits. On a phone the light swings down-left instead.\n\nSuccess: over 1.7 seconds the jets lock, the bands straighten into even stripes, and a faint green edge appears on the lit rim.\n\nPalette: #0B0D13, #1C2230, #3A4253, #6A7079, #A7A59C, #E2DDCF; haze #93C4D4; green #C6F135; background #07080B.\n\nRuns on the graphics chip at about 2.4% CPU idle; needs WebGL 2. The button never changes colour and reads \"Logging in\".",
                      "lineHeight": 1.45,
                      "fontFamily": "Schibsted Grotesk",
                      "fontSize": 20,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
